The buzzer sounded. Tim opened the chain-link gate and stepped onto the field. The grass dampened his shoes. There was a hint of fryer oil in the air. Sabana, wearing number 12, stood ahead. Her headband was loose and her expression was serious. She moved decisively, going past anyone in her path. Sweat ran down her hair. Away from the field, she wore her hoodie and kept to herself. On the pitch, she was focused and assertive, moving with purpose and determination.

Tim traced the knot in her jaw after she fumbled the tackle—a flash of anger sparking beneath the surface before she buried it, springing back up as if pain were theoretical, something for other bodies. In him, pride and regret tangled: he wanted to yell her name, but the last thing she’d want was attention from him. That morning, she'd swept into the kitchen, shoulder-checking him with a grin, teasing him about his age until both of them laughed. Last week, he’d let her down—crawling through highway gridlock, wishing he could reverse time and show up. But today, he was here, silent in his resolve, noticing every gap between them, knowing he’d built those distances even as she’d stopped trying to bridge them.